    "notes": "The alert contains four traces that all share one root cause (unchecked FileUtils.resolveFile(dir, entryName) result at Expand.java:153) fanning out to four sinks at Expand.java lines 178, 164/169, and 204. I targeted the FileOutputStream write sink (line 178) for the primary POV because it gives the cleanest, most unambiguous, deterministic evidence: a real file with attacker-chosen content lands on disk outside the destination directory, verifiable by simple existence+content checks without relying on timing or filesystem metadata. I validated the POV's discriminating power by temporarily patching Expand.java in this worktree with a canonical-path containment check (dir.getCanonicalPath()-prefix guard before the try block), confirming the same command flips from exit 0 (vulnerable) to exit 1 (blocked), then reverted the product file back to its original unpatched state (verified via `git status --porcelain` showing only the new `.security-pipeline/` directory as untracked, no modifications to tracked files). The other three sinks (164/169 mkdirs, 204 setLastModified) are not separately exercised by this POV but are documented in `evidence`/`regression_hint` so the patcher does not fix only the write path.",
    "pov_command": "mvn -B -q -DskipTests package && rm -rf target/pov-classes && mkdir -p target/pov-classes && javac -cp target/classes -d target/pov-classes .security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPov.java && java -cp target/classes:target/pov-classes ZipSlipPov",
    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPov.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce: for every zip entry, the canonicalized/normalized extraction target File must be a descendant of the canonicalized destination directory (`dir`) before ANY filesystem operation (mkdirs, FileOutputStream, setLastModified) is performed on it; if it is not, extraction of that entry must be rejected (throw) rather than silently clamped, redirected, or skipped-but-continuing to process other sinks with the same unchecked path. The check must be applied once at/near Expand.java:153 (right after `resolveFile`) so it covers all four sinks (164/169/178/204) rather than patching only the FileOutputStream call \u2014 a partial fix that only guards line 178 would still leave directory-creation (164/169) and timestamp-tampering (204) exploitable. Known bypass variants the fix must also block: (a) simple parent traversal `../foo` (this POV) and multi-segment `../../../foo`; (b) traversal disguised as a directory entry (`../evilDir/`) that only hits the mkdirs sinks (164/169), not the write sink; (c) absolute-path entry names (e.g. `/etc/passwd` or on Windows `C:\\...`), which `FileUtils.resolveFile` explicitly special-cases as root-relative absolute paths (FileUtils.java ~1378-1394) and would bypass a naive \"startsWith(dest)\" check if resolveFile itself isn't also constrained; (d) backslash-encoded traversal (`..\\\\foo`) since resolveFile normalizes separators; (e) symlink-based escapes where an entry name is benign but a pre-existing symlink inside dest points outside \u2014 canonicalization (getCanonicalPath) rather than mere string-prefix comparison on the absolute path is needed to catch this; (f) zip-slip via entries that differ only in case or trailing slashes on case-insensitive filesystems. A regression test should assert both that `../pwned.txt`-style entries are rejected/contained AND that a benign entry like `sub/dir/file.txt` still extracts normally inside dest.",
